See you.

00:01:33.929 --> 00:01:36.779
- Together with Kenny Lerer, who
is was a good friend of Gary's,

00:01:36.779 --> 00:01:40.593
I launched the
Huffington Post in 2005.

00:01:40.593 --> 00:01:41.228
- That little thing.

00:01:41.228 --> 00:01:44.147
- We are now 11 years old.
- Unbelievable, huh?

00:01:44.147 --> 00:01:45.194
What a run that was.

00:01:45.194 --> 00:01:46.613
- Amazing.
- You know before we get into

00:01:46.613 --> 00:01:48.767
questions I'm 
curious for my own self

00:01:49.997 --> 00:01:53.247
when you guys launched
that, did it feel like yes

00:01:53.247 --> 00:01:55.872
did you know, did you not know?

00:01:55.872 --> 00:01:56.825
Did it get much
bigger than you thought?

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:59.092
Is it still not as
big as you thought?

00:01:59.092 --> 00:02:00.933
What was your
real point of view?

00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:05.338
- We were very excited about
taking the conversation that was

00:02:05.338 --> 00:02:08.500
going on among really
interesting people, some known,

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.589
some not known and
moving it online.

00:02:10.589 --> 00:02:12.258
Believe it or not,

00:02:12.258 --> 00:02:16.398
2005 bloggers were 
still dismissed as people

00:02:16.398 --> 00:02:21.250
who couldn't get a job writing
in their parents' basement.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.490
- Yes.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.655
- And we change the paradigm.

00:02:24.655 --> 00:02:29.136
On our first day we had 
Larry David and Ellen Degeneres

00:02:29.136 --> 00:02:32.042
and Walter Cronkite and
Arthur Schlesinger.

00:02:32.042 --> 00:02:35.652
People who could have written an
op-ed in the New York Times.

00:02:35.652 --> 00:02:36.543
- That's right.

00:02:36.543 --> 00:02:39.026
- But chose to write on HuffPost
and discovered how much easier

00:02:39.026 --> 00:02:43.649
it was to actually write
something online see it

00:02:43.649 --> 00:02:48.374
immediately posted not having to
deal with editors and editing.

00:02:49.864 --> 00:02:53.627
And it just begun to change the
perception around blogging and

00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:58.341
at the same time that we very
proudly were a platform,

00:02:58.341 --> 00:03:00.683
that has grown of 
course exponentially,

00:03:00.683 --> 00:03:04.874
we wanted to be a journalistic
enterprise that ended up winning

00:03:04.874 --> 00:03:08.793
a Pulitzer we now have over 800
journalists, reporters, editors

00:03:08.793 --> 00:03:12.124
in 15 countries soon 16.

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:17.478
So whenever you start something
I don't know if you ever really

00:03:17.478 --> 00:03:19.171
know where it's going to go.

00:03:19.171 --> 00:03:23.223
Because the fun of it is that
you invent it as you go along.

00:03:23.223 --> 00:03:25.114
- But you have intuitions.

00:03:25.114 --> 00:03:27.868
- Yeah you have intuitions, you
trust that this is, for me it's

00:03:27.868 --> 00:03:30.937
all about are you tapping
into something in the zeitgeist.

00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:31.931
- I think that's right.

00:03:31.931 --> 00:03:35.259
- If you're tap into something,
you have the wind on your back

00:03:35.259 --> 00:03:36.878
and it's much easier.

00:03:36.878 --> 00:03:39.469
- This sleep platform, The Sleep
Revolution the book that came

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:41.747
out was that tapping
into the zeitgeist?

00:03:41.747 --> 00:03:42.806
Was this a passion?

00:03:42.806 --> 00:03:44.995
Where did this come from?

00:03:44.995 --> 00:03:49.666
- It definitely is tapping into
the zeitgeist but it came from a

00:03:49.666 --> 00:03:53.519
terrible wake-up call when I
collapsed from sleep deprivation

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:57.109
and burnout and hit my 
head on the way down

00:03:57.109 --> 00:04:00.359
on my desk, broke my cheekbone

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:03.676
and that was the beginning 
of my but literally

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:07.926
coming to in a pool of blood
and having to ask myself is this

00:04:07.926 --> 00:04:09.903
really what success looks like?
- Right.

00:04:09.903 --> 00:04:14.596
- And realizing not just I but
millions of people have bought

00:04:14.596 --> 00:04:15.862
into this collective--

00:04:15.862 --> 00:04:17.612
- How much were you
sleeping at that point?

00:04:17.612 --> 00:04:20.887
I was sleeping 4 to 5 hours.

00:04:20.887 --> 00:04:22.887
- A night?
- Yes.

00:04:24.547 --> 00:04:27.956
Building the Huffington Post it
was two years old and I had two

00:04:27.956 --> 00:04:29.662
teenage daughters and--

00:04:29.662 --> 00:04:32.758
- So this was in 2007?
- 2007, yes.

00:04:32.758 --> 00:04:37.212
And the precipitating
circumstance of my collapse was

00:04:37.212 --> 00:04:40.902
having been around colleges with
my daughter for her to decide

00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:44.355
what college she
wanted to go to.

00:04:44.355 --> 00:04:46.165
- Yes.

00:04:46.165 --> 00:04:50.037
- What happened is that we're
basically together during the

00:04:50.037 --> 00:04:54.307
day no Blackberries that was the
ground rule and then at night

00:04:54.307 --> 00:04:55.747
she would go to sleep
and I would start working.

00:04:58.107 --> 00:05:02.270
And I feel there's been

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.490
so much 
misinformation about sleep.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:08.059
Going back to the Industrial
Revolution when we started

00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:11.369
thinking human beings can
be treated like machines to

00:05:11.369 --> 00:05:12.622
minimize downtime.

00:05:12.622 --> 00:05:13.893
- I think I am a machine.

00:05:13.893 --> 00:05:14.929
- You are a machine.

00:05:14.929 --> 00:05:17.213
- But, but and
people know I work a lot.

00:05:17.213 --> 00:05:21.394
I, and I say this a lot,
I'm a huge believer in sleep.

00:05:21.394 --> 00:05:25.346
I always thought about hustling
18 hours a day and throwing

00:05:25.346 --> 00:05:26.363
those kinds of crazy numbers

00:05:26.363 --> 00:05:29.460
out but boy I am like 
all in on sleep.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.714
It's just math in the same way
of like I started taking care of

00:05:31.714 --> 00:05:33.056
my health a couple years ago.

00:05:33.056 --> 00:05:34.362
You need to sleep.

00:05:34.362 --> 00:05:36.076
I don't think anybody 
has won the game.

00:05:36.076 --> 00:05:40.271
For example, I'm really curious.

00:05:40.271 --> 00:05:42.465
I think a lot people head 4 to
5 from you, a lot of them were

00:05:42.465 --> 00:05:43.804
like, "Oh that's
what Gary sleeps."

00:05:43.804 --> 00:05:48.637
I'm rarely five, mainly six
sometimes seven it's when

00:05:48.637 --> 00:05:53.351
I travel I can get caught and
I'm a much bigger fan of sleep

00:05:53.351 --> 00:05:54.746
I think than people realize.

00:05:54.746 --> 00:05:59.362
- And now it's you, it's
athletes a lot of athletes

00:05:59.362 --> 00:06:03.144
talking about how when they
get enough sleep it makes them

00:06:03.144 --> 00:06:06.226
better on the
court or in the field.

00:06:06.226 --> 00:06:09.708
I have a whole section here on
Andre Iguodala and how when he

00:06:09.708 --> 00:06:13.542
went to eight hours sleep his
stats dramatically improve.

00:06:13.542 --> 00:06:17.990
Lebron James, Rogers Federer
when he plays Wimbledon

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.744
he rents two houses.

00:06:19.744 --> 00:06:24.145
One for his wife and young
children and one for himself

00:06:24.145 --> 00:06:27.738
because he says nothing can
disturb my sleep the night

00:06:27.738 --> 00:06:30.227
before game day.

00:06:32.417 --> 00:06:34.907
People may wonder how
much sleep do they need.

00:06:35.797 --> 00:06:39.025
So here's the thing you
may have a genetic mutation.

00:06:39.025 --> 00:06:41.337
If you have a genetic mutation

00:06:41.337 --> 00:06:44.869
you can get away 
with 4 to 6 hours.

00:06:44.869 --> 00:06:48.719
Frankly, the rest of us, 
the vast majority of us,

00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:51.925
need 7 to 9 hours.
- Wow.

00:06:51.925 --> 00:06:55.395
- You may be seven,
you may be nine--

00:06:55.395 --> 00:06:58.055
- Well Staphon is 10.
(laughter)

00:06:58.055 --> 00:07:00.241
- He looks fabulous.

00:07:00.241 --> 00:07:02.958
Maybe that's what it takes.

00:07:02.958 --> 00:07:06.459
- Alright India, I apologize
let's get into some questions

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:07.677
and we'll keep jamming.

00:07:07.677 --> 00:07:10.102
Oh, a video?
I love it.

00:07:10.102 --> 00:07:14.113
They're all video except one.
Okay. Great.

00:07:14.113 --> 00:07:15.482
- Hey Arianna and GaryVee.

00:07:15.482 --> 00:07:17.885
Arianna it was so good
to meet you last week.

